1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Welcome back. Today I want to talk about whether compute scaling still
matters for frontier capability.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:14,000
My position in March was simple: the timelines people quote are wildly
overhyped, and we are nowhere near a country of geniuses in a datacenter.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,000
I said then that scaling training compute was hitting diminishing returns,
and that efficiency gains would not close the gap on their own.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Since then I have changed my mind on one specific point, and I want to be
explicit about it rather than quietly revise the record.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Inference-time compute turned out to matter far more than I expected. That
is a real update, and it came from watching reasoning models improve.

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:48,000
What I still believe: regulation written around vague terms like
catastrophic risk can be repurposed to suppress models that criticize
government policy.

7
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Those terms are not precise. National security language in particular has
a long history of being stretched well past its original scope.

8
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:05,000
So if you hear me say later that such language is precise enough to prevent
abuse, hold me to this clip.

9
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Next week my guest argues the opposite of everything I just said about
scaling. I am not going to pretend I agree with her.

10
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
That is the whole episode. If you want the longer argument, the March video
is linked below.
